import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="News"
  type="Product"
  title="nomue verifier Release 1 candidate is now public"
  description="The pre-release local verifier is available for public inspection and scoped fixture-based testing."
  published="August 16, 2026"
  updated="August 23, 2026"
  status="Pre-release — package 0.2.1-rc.0"
>
  <section>
    <h2>What is available</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier repository</a>
      contains a pre-release candidate package for local, issuer-independent verification
      of scoped nomue Record properties.
    </p>
    <p>
      The package can check Record structure and semantics, content-digest integrity,
      declared profile admissibility, and the registered Welch result fields under the
      exact candidate bundle. Verification runs locally after dependencies are installed;
      it does not call a Licklider server or execute Record-supplied code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Release status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p><strong>This is a pre-release candidate, not Protocol Release 1.</strong></p>
      <p>
        Package version <code>0.2.1-rc.0</code> is aligned to the planned Release 1
        support target. nomue Protocol Release 1 has not been published, and the final
        candidate source pin has not been fixed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The earlier headline “nomue verifier released” was too broad for this maturity.
      This page replaces it with the exact public state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Try the two public fixtures</h2>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier.git
cd nomue-verifier
npm install

npm exec -- nomue verify records/valid.json --format json
npm exec -- nomue verify records/invalid-result-mismatch.json --format json</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first fixture is expected to complete with all applicable scoped checks passing.
      The second contains a declared-result mismatch and is expected to return a failed
      scoped check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a clean report does not mean</h2>
    <p>A clean verifier report does not establish:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>scientific truth or scientific validity;</li>
      <li>that input data or researcher declarations are truthful;</li>
      <li>causal validity or publication acceptance;</li>
      <li>correctness outside the exact registered candidate bundle; or</li>
      <li>production attestation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is no single overall <code>VERIFIED</code> result. Consumers that need exact
      outcomes must parse the machine-readable report and retain its stated boundaries.
    </p>
  </section>

</PublicationLayout>
